s security after the contingency by taking corrective action**: **N-1 corrective**. It is *not* a no-action-after-trip solution.\n\n| Option | Cost | Line-trip security result |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Cheapest | \u20ac26.48/h | Not N-1 secure: 29 of 41 outage screens had violations. |\n| Least disturbance | \u20ac111.99/h | Not N-1 secure: 29 of 41 outage screens had violations. |\n| N-1 preventive | \u20ac784.00/h | Not secure for all studied trips even before any trip response: 5 of the 8 studied outages remained unsecured. |\n| **N-1 corrective** | **\u20ac276.54/h** | **A checked corrective plan clears all violations for the 8 studied critical outages; 4 plans use demand response and/or load relief.** |\n\nThe corrective option moves **58.38 MW** and **47.40 MVAr** before a trip, with **146.55 MW** of external-grid import. Its post-trip plans were checked for these lines: **L35, L0, L1, L3, L29, L37, L36 and L4**. The corrective actions include generator redispatch and, for some outages, demand response or load relief.\n\n**Scope caveat:** this does not establish security for every possible line trip. The full screen still found violations in **19 of 41** outages before corrective action; only the 8 critical outages received detailed corrective plans, and the trip of **L33** was excluded because it leaves Bus_25 without supply. Costs use a mix of **synthetic and case-file prices**. The operator must weigh the options; the study does not choose one.\n\n\ud83d\udccb Data source: measurements"}
